  "id": "sec-streamfunction",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-streamfunction.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "4.2",
  "title": "The streamfunction",
  "body": " The streamfunction   Our next task is to introduce the concept of the streamfunction . Remember that in 2D, the irrotational flow led to the equation and this led to the existence of the potential function. If we begin with incompressibility, however, we have , which can be written as We can therefore deduce the existence of an analogous function, the streamfunction , , satisfying Alternatively and more conveniently, we can write To establish the existence of the streamfunction, we follow a similar proof as in but now with the definition that where again is an arbitrary function of . The proof is otherwise identical, relying on establishing the independence of path of the integral using Stokes' theorem.  Why all this work? The streamfunction has an intuitive interpretation via the following result.   The streamfunction is constant along streamlines   The streamfunction, , is constant along streamlines of the flow (i.e. curves that are everywhere tangent to at a fixed time ).    The proof follows simply by the fact that where the first equality uses .  Since is normal to the level sets of , and is orthogonal to , the velocity is tangent to the level sets. These level sets are therefore streamlines.    A graphical depiction of the property in is shown below.   Flow between two streamlines, the streamlines and . Later, we will consider the flux through the contour illustrated in the figure along with its unit normal .     The streamfunction is thus constant on streamlines. Consider two streamlines. The following theorem relates the flux between the streamlines to the values of on them.   Flux between streamlines   Consider two streamlines and . We assume that the streamlines pass through the points A and B respectively. Consider a contour connecting A and B. The flux (net flow of fluid) through and hence between the streamlines is We have not specified the sign of the flux as it depends on the direction chosen through .    By definition, the flux is given by the integral where is any smooth path joining the two streamlines with unit normal , as shown in .  Note that given the curve, and in consideration of a small arclength element , the tangent and normal are given by Therefore we can write . Then the flux is re-written as the following: In the third line above, is the change of along the contour. Note that there is a somewhat arbitrary choice of direction for the contour , as related to the selection of the normal direction , and the positivity or negativity of the flux. To be safe, we have taken the absolute value in the statement of the theorem.    An example of the use of the above theorem is given in .   Flux from the streamfunction   Let the velocity of a two-dimensional flow be given by the formula where is a positive constant. By finding the streamfunction, , calculate the volume flux across a curve connecting points and using .    We can check, using the integration of velocity components that Hence the flux is We can verify this flux should be positive if the normal is oriented to the right of the curve from A to B.    Finally, note that the velocity potential was governed by Laplace's equation, . The streamfunction is also governed by the same Laplace's equation.   Streamfunction satisfies Laplace's equation   If the flow is also irrotational, then, like the velocity potential, the streamfunction satisfies Laplace's equation:     Substitute the relationship into .    For some flows, such as the line source in , it is easier to work in polar coordinates. Since we know that by , we can use the conversion of the curl to polar coordinates in to give which allows us to relate the radial and angular velocities to the streamfunction.    Elementary flows  Let us return to each of the examples in and reconsider their corresponding streamfunctions.   Uniform flow  uniform flow, streamfunction   Find the streamfunction for the uniform flow of , and sketch its streamlines.    Integrating and gives, up to an arbitrary constant, Lines of constant are , as in .     Stagnation point flow stagnation point flow, streamfunction   Find the streamfunction for the stagnation point flow of , and sketch its streamlines.    Integrating and gives, up to an arbitrary constant, . Curves of constant are the hyperbolae shown in .     Line source  line source, streamfunction   Find the streamfunction for the line source of , and sketch its streamlines.    From , the velocity is entirely radial, Using , we integrate (with ) to give The lines of constant are the radial lines of constant , as in .    Note that is a multi-valued function in , gaining a jump of every time the origin is encircled. This is a warning that the standard proof, analogous to , leading to the existence of a unique streamfunction via , would not apply, because the flow domain (the plane with the origin removed) is not simply connected. However, despite this, the streamfunction provides well-defined predictions of streamlines on the cut plane with e.g. .   Line source in a uniform flow   Find the streamfunction for the line source in a uniform flow of , and the equation of its streamlines.    By linearity, we add the streamfunctions of the uniform flow (with ) and the line source: The streamlines are given by having chosen the constant on the right-hand side for convenience. Then using , we have     Our previous examples were reliant on considering the flows generated by the velocity potentials studied previously. However, we can also consider \"fundamental solutions\" of the equation in their own right. Recall that in deriving the velocity potential of the line source in , we considered a radially symmetric problem, where is only dependent on the distance from the origin. The same argument shows that a radially symmetric streamfunction is also a solution. This leads us to the following example.   Line vortex  line vortex   The radially symmetric streamfunction defined up to a constant, corresponds to a line vortex . Find its velocity , and compute the circulation around a circle of radius centred at the origin.    The streamlines are circles of constant , shown in . From , so the fluid orbits the origin, with speed increasing as .   Streamlines (or velocity field) of a line vortex with .    The circulation around a contour is i.e. the sum of the velocity components tangential to . By Stokes' theorem it equals the flux of vorticity through any surface bounded by . Since the flow is irrotational away from the origin, all of this vorticity is concentrated at the origin. On the circle , , so The quantity is called the vortex strength , analogous to the source strength in . Notice that corresponds to flow in the anticlockwise sense, and to flow in the clockwise sense.    In , you will practise the computation of fluxes using line integrals.   "
