  "id": "sec-complex-potential",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-complex-potential.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "4.3",
  "title": "The complex potential",
  "body": " The complex potential    Changed in 2026  This section was streamlined and shortened.   In the previous two sections, we studied the velocity potential and streamfunction of two-dimensional potential flows using real-valued Vector Calculus. You may have noticed a certain symmetry between the formulae for and . Complex analysis makes this symmetry transparent, and provides a much more elegant and powerful framework for studying two-dimensional flow.   Examinable content  The proofs in this section are non-examinable, but you are expected to understand the theorems and their relevance to potential flow.     Analytic functions and Laplace's equation  We write a complex function as , where and and are the real and imaginary parts of , and use subscripts for partial derivatives, e.g. . The derivative is defined as for real functions, with the crucial difference that the limit must exist, and take the same value, however in the complex plane. For further details, see and .   Analyticity  analytic   A function is analytic in a domain if it is differentiable at every point of the domain.     Cauchy-Riemann equations   If is analytic in a domain, then the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold in that domain. Conversely, if and have continuous first partial derivatives satisfying , then is analytic.    Proof (non-examinable)  Approaching along the real direction, , the definition gives . Approaching along the imaginary direction, , gives . Equating the real and imaginary parts of the two results yields the Cauchy-Riemann equations. For the converse, see .     Analyticity and Laplace's equation   If is analytic in a domain , then and satisfy Laplace's equation, in .    Proof (non-examinable)  Analytic functions are infinitely differentiable (see ), so we may differentiate to obtain The second derivatives are continuous, so , and therefore . Laplace's equation is analogously proved for .    This remarkable result is a major reason why complex analysis played such an important role in applied mathematics and physics, since Laplace's equation governs gravitation, electrostatics, fluid mechanics, and more. In particular, any function that is analytic in a region, whether a polynomial, exponential, logarithm, or otherwise, can be associated with (some kind of) potential flow there.    Examples of elementary flows  Let us return to the examples of flows studied in and re-interpret them using the theory of analytic functions.  We will associate the velocity potential, , and streamfunction with an analytic function in the following way.   Complex potential   Let and be the respective velocity potential and streamfunction for some potential flow. We define and call the complex potential . Since and , the functions and satisfy the Cauchy-Riemann equations, so is analytic.  Note that by differentiating in the horizontal and vertical directions, we have Therefore it follows that the horizontal and vertical velocity components of the flow, related via , are given by      Uniform flow  uniform flow, complex   Show that the complex potential of the uniform flow of is , and recover , and .    With , whose real and imaginary parts are the and of and the corresponding example in . Also , so .     Stagnation point flow  stagnation point flow, complex   Show that the complex potential of the stagnation point flow of is , and recover , and .    We have , so and . Also , so .     Line source  line source, complex   Show that the complex potential of the line source of is and recover , and .    The complex logarithm is not analytic at , a branch point , but is analytic away from the origin once a branch cut is chosen. Writing in polar form, it is evaluated via Hence Indeed, the streamlines are the rays . From , multiplying the top and bottom by the conjugate of ,      Line vortex flow  line vortex, complex   Show that the complex potential of the line vortex of is and recover , and .    Using with , so and , which is constant on circles . The velocity follows from so , i.e. , as in .     "
