  "title": "Stokes waves",
  "body": " Stokes waves   Linearisation of the water-wave equations  Our task in this section is to linearise the water-wave equations given in . The culprit to the difficulty in seeking solutions concerns two main issues. The first is the nonlinearity in Bernoulli's equation (i.e. the term ). The second is to do with the specification of the unknown free surface, , and the need to solve the extra equations on this unknown location.  The idea of linearisation relies upon the consideration that if disturbances in the fluid are small, then the quadratic terms in the free-surface conditions can be neglected. One way to see this is to introduce a scaling on all the associated quantities. For example, we consider the free surface as written as where ( is a small number). The parameter is simply a notational device to help remind us of the smallness of the free surface.  Now since we assume the motion in the fluid is also small, then we introduce a similar scaling on the potential function. We assume within the fluid. As a consequence, all velocities are small and of order .  Now consider the evaluation of the vertical velocity on the surface. By Taylor's formula, we can expand: In fact, if we use our assumptions and , we can see the size of the vertical velocity more explicitly: We will essentially ignore all terms of or smaller.  Let us consider now the kinematic condition . Since the term with the horizontal velocity , then we see that the kinematic approximation now yields As we indicated, the use of is only to aid with ordering. We see that if we return back to the original variable scalings, then the above indicates that the approximate kinematic condition to be considered is: It is typical, when dealing with linear water waves, to dispense with the notation indicating \"approximate\", i.e. here, on the understanding that the solutions are satisfied only approximately.   The development of the linearised equation(s) seem quite lengthy, as presented above, but with understanding, you will see that they are quite simple. In essence, you have replaced the exact free-surface condition of with on the assumption the flow is linearised about and .   You will be able to practice this linearisation in the exercises.  The linearisation of Bernoulli's equation in is done similarly, and the end result is that the term, is assumed to be small compared to the other terms of the equation.  Using the linearisation techniques, similar to the kinematic equation and its linear variation , Bernoulli's equation in is then approximated as   This yields the set of governing linear equations as follows.   Linear water wave equations   Consider two-dimensional potential flow (inviscid, incompressible, irrotational) of fluid. A model for linear water waves is as follows: where the free surface is located at .  In water of infinite depth, we require that while for the case of finite depth, we have       Solving for linear waves in infinite depth  We now seek to solve the linear water wave equations in .  We assume that the wave takes the form of a simple harmonic wave: Examining the free-surface conditions in and suggests that the potential should take the form of a sinusoidal: which we shall verify is appropriate.  Firstly, substitution of into Laplace's equation yields   Turning now to the two free-surface conditions and , as well as the condition of infinite depth, we have the following necessary constraints:   The solution of yields and so the infinite depth condition yields . We substitute now into the two remaining conditions and simplify, yielding the matrix equation:   Therefore, in order for there to be nontrivial solutions, the determinant of the matrix must be zero, and this yields or alternatively if we take the positive root,   The above expression is known as the dispersion relation for water waves in deep water.   "
