  "title": "Reynolds number estimation.",
  "body": " Reynolds number estimation    Based on Problem 2.1 in .   Give an order-of-magnitude estimate of the Reynolds number in each of the following situations:     flow past the wing of a jumbo jet at 150 m\/s (roughly half the speed of sound);    The kinematic viscosity of air at standard temperature and pressure is about . The wingspan of a jumbo jet is about , so the Reynolds number is approximately       blood flow in a large artery (diameter 2 cm) at 0.3 m\/s;    The kinematic viscosity of blood is about . The diameter of the artery is about , so the Reynolds number is approximately Although this is above 2000, blood flow in arteries is typically laminar.      a thick layer of honey draining off a spoon at 1 cm\/s;    The kinematic viscosity of honey is about . The thickness of the layer is about , so the Reynolds number is approximately       a spermatozoon with tail length m swimming at 100 m\/s in water.    The kinematic viscosity of water at room temperature is about . The length of the tail is about , so the Reynolds number is approximately     "
