  "title": "Analysis of flow in a channel.",
  "body": " Analysis of flow in a channel   A viscous incompressible fluid flows steadily between two stationary rigid boundaries at under a constant pressure gradient .     Write down the boundary conditions to apply at each of the plates.    The no-slip boundary conditions give for all and .      The velocity is steady and therefore independent of . Explain why it is also reasonable to assume that the velocity profile is independent of and , and also that the -component of the velocity is zero .    Since the plates are infinite in the and directions, and the pressure gradient is constant, there is no preferred location in these directions, and hence the velocity profile is independent of and . Furthermore, since there is no pressure gradient in the direction, there is no driving force for flow in this direction, and hence the -component of the velocity is zero ( ).      Use the continuity equation to show that the -component of the velocity is also zero ( ).    The continuity equation for an incompressible fluid is Since the velocity is independent of and , the first and third terms are zero, and hence Since the plates are rigid boundaries, there is no flow through them, and hence the no-penetration boundary conditions give for all and . Since is independent of and zero at both and , it follows that for all .      Hence the only non-zero component of the velocity is the -component, , and this only depends on , . Check that the profile satisfies the boundary conditions.    At , we have and at , we have Hence the profile satisfies the boundary conditions.      The velocity profile increases as increases and decreases as increases. Explain why this is to be expected. Thus, although you cannot yet prove this is the correct velocity profile (you will soon be able to do so), this profile does look reasonable from what you already know.    A larger pressure gradient means a larger driving force for the flow, and hence a larger velocity. A larger viscosity means a larger resistance to the flow, and hence a smaller velocity. Therefore, it is to be expected that the velocity profile increases as increases and decreases as increases.      Calculate the three components of the stress vector, the force per unit area that the fluid exerts, on each of the two plates. You may assume that the pressure equals at .    The pressure at a general point is given by   At , the normal stress is given by the pressure, pointing into the plate: (note the negative sign as \"into the plate\" is in the -direction). The -component of the shear stress at is given by (note that this is positive since the fluid is pushing the plate in the positive -direction). The -component of the shear stress at is zero since . Hence   At , the normal stress is given by the pressure, pointing into the plate: (note the sign change compared to the previous part as \"into the plate\" is in the -direction). The -component of the shear stress at is given by Note that the formula used has a negative sign as \"into the fluid\" is in the -direction. The overall sign is positive since the fluid is pushing the plate in the positive -direction. (It is good to check signs after this kind of calculation by checking the direction of the force.) The -component of the shear stress at is zero since . Hence       Compare the stresses on the two plates. Why is this to be expected?    The normal stresses on the two plates are equal in magnitude but opposite in direction, as expected since the pressure acts equally on both plates, but \"into the plates\" is a different direction.  The shear stresses on the two plates are equal in magnitude and direction, indicating that the fluid is exerting the same tangential force on both plates in the direction of the flow. This is also expected since the plane is a plane of symmetry.    "
