  "body": " Oscillating flow in a pipe   In this exercise, you will derive the equations for flow in a circular pipe, driven by an oscillating pressure gradient      In cylindrical polar coordinates the continuity equation is and the Navier Stokes equations are where the Laplacian is defined by its action on a function : and represents the body forces per unit mass acting on the fluid.  Stating your assumptions, use the continuity equation to show that is everywhere zero.    We assume that the flow is axisymmetric and fully developed. The continuity equation reduces to which implies that where is independent of . Regularity at implies that , and therefore everywhere.      Simplify the Navier Stokes equations, and use them to show that where You may neglect gravity.    With the assumptions, the Navier Stokes equations reduce to There is thus one non-trivial component, and, taking the part proportional to , we have which, dividing by , is equivalent to Rearranging gives the result.      Make the substitution , where , to obtain the canonical form     With this substitution, we have and hence giving       Find a particular integral to this equation, i.e. any solution that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation    For simplicity, seek a constant solution, and we find       The complementary function can be written in terms of a special function of which you are probably not aware called a Bessel function . Regularity implies that we pick the Bessel functions of the the first kind, which are shown in .  Examples of the solution for three different Womersley numbers are shown in , and . The solution for small Womersley numbers resembles the Poiseuille flow profile, while that for large Womersley numbers is plug (uniform) flow across the core of the pipe with boundary layers exhibiting rapid spatial oscillations.   Womersley flow profile for .   Womersley     Womersley flow profile for .   Womersley     Womersley flow profile for .   Womersley      "
