  "number": "6.11.3",
  "title": "Wavy-walled pipe.",
  "body": " Wavy-walled pipe   A long cylindrical pipe with slowly varying radius is aligned with its axis pointing in the -direction. A much narrower cylinder of constant radius is placed coaxially within the larger cylinder. The narrow cylinder has porous walls and emits fluid at a constant velocity perpendicular to its surface. A fluid flows steadily in the gap between the two cylinders.   Wavy-walled pipe with inner cylinder that emits fluid.    In this question, you will work in cylindrical polar coodinates , centred on the axis of the pipe.  Despite the fact that there is flow out from the inner cylinder in the radial direction, you may assume that the flow in the annular gap between the cylinders is unidirectional along the pipe in the -direction. (In reality there will be a thin layer next to the inner cylinder in which this is not true.) Furthermore, you may assume that the flow is uniform across the cross-section, that is .     Consider a narrow slice of the cylinder between and . Find an expression for the volumetric flux of fluid through the surfaces and .     Wavy-walled pipe planes to apply mass conservation.    The volumetric flux into the left-hand surface of this region equals area times velocity: and that out of the right-hand surface equals where we used the chain rule to rewrite the increments in terms of . Note that throughout this equation, we neglect and higher powers, as they are small.      Now find an expression for the volumetric flux of fluid through the section of the inner cylinder between and .    The area of the inner cylinder in this region is , and thus we have a volumetric flux entering this region from the inner cylinder.      Now use the principle of mass conservation to show that   Note that you can actually solve this equation by instead considering the volume between and . This volume has four surfaces: the inner cylinder surface , the outer cylinder surface , and the two cross-sectional surfaces at and . The inner cylinder surface gives a volume flux in of , the outer cylinder surface gives no volume flux, the surface gives a volume flux in of , and the surface gives a volume flux out of . Equating total volume flux in and volume flux out gives and hence This is true for all , hence (setting ) This was not asked for in this question.    Since the volume of the pipe between and is fixed, the total volumetric flux into this region equals the total volumetric flux out.  Thus thus proving the result.      Write down an expression for the Reynolds number of this flow, explaining your reasoning. You may assume that the pipe has length and that is a typical value of . Your answer should be in terms of the problem parameters , , , , and .    A suitable lengthscale is . Simplifying the differential equation for , by neglecting variations in pipe radius and the radius of the inner cylinder , gives suggesting a suitable velocity scale is this times (the velocity that would be attained at the end of the pipe): Hence the Reynolds number is   Many students did not find an expression for the velocity scale, and just left it as or similar. This would mean that we do not have an expression for the Reynolds number in terms of the problem parameters. Also, many students did not approximate as , which simplifies the expression (some did get , but you can go further than this).  I have added a final sentence to the question to clarify that the answer should be in terms of the problem parameters as very few students attempted to find an order of magnitude for .      State in a sentence the conditions under which it might be appropriate to assume that the velocity is uniform across the cross section. You do not need to do a calculation.    The Reynolds number would need to be large (but not so large that the flow becomes turbulent).  In the limit of large Reynolds number, we would recover the Euler equation from the Navier Stokes equation (see ). In this limit we only need a no-penetration condition ( ) and not the full no-slip conditions ( ), and so in this case uniform flow is possible.      In reality, in a sentence, what would you observe in the flow near the outer wall?    The flow would need to satisfy the no-slip boundary condition at the outer cylinder and the velocity there would therefore tend to zero.    "
